# {{ name }}

{{ spec.title }} — spec-first, hexagonal architecture, Connexion 3 (ASGI). Created from
service-template{% if template_repo %} (`{{ template_repo }}`){% endif %}; the generator lives
in `tools/service_gen/` and is driven by `openapi/openapi.yml`.

## Run

Everything runs in Docker via `./dev.sh`; no local Python needed.

```sh
./dev.sh up            # app on http://localhost:8080{{ spec.base_path }}/ui + postgres on :5432
./dev.sh docs          # open Swagger UI
./dev.sh migrate       # apply Alembic migrations
./dev.sh test          # pytest
./dev.sh check         # what CI runs: generated-code drift, ruff + spec lint + layer rules, mypy, pytest, migrations
./dev.sh fresh         # wipe the database volume and start over
./dev.sh shell         # bash inside the app container
./dev.sh psql          # psql into the dev database
./dev.sh help          # everything else
```

`./dev.sh` works from the host and from inside the app container (e.g. a VS Code
dev container). Inside, `compose.yaml` sets `IN_APP_CONTAINER=1` and commands run
directly; from the host they run in a one-off app container. Commands that manage
containers (`up`, `down`, `build`, `logs`, `fresh`, `psql`, `init`) run from the host.

`./dev.sh up` hot-reloads on source changes (the repo is bind-mounted into the
container). Dependencies are plain `pip` from `requirements.txt` (runtime) and
`requirements-dev.txt` (tooling); after changing them run `./dev.sh build`.

Settings are read from the environment with the `APP_` prefix (see
`{{ package }}/settings.py`); `compose.yaml` sets `APP_DATABASE_URL` for the dev database.
Copy `.env.example` to `.env` to change host ports or the log level.

## Layout

```
openapi/openapi.yml            the contract; edit this first
{{ package }}/
  __main__.py                  `python -m {{ package }} serve | run-due-jobs`
  main.py                      composition root for the HTTP server: services -> adapters -> app
  worker.py                    composition root for the job runner (no web framework)
  wiring.py                    what both share: engine, session factory, clock
  settings.py
  generated/                   OWNED BY service-gen, never edit
    models.py                  Pydantic DTOs for every schema
    handlers/                  one class per tag; validate -> adapter -> serialise
  adapters/
    inbound/<tag>_adapter.py   DTO -> domain object -> port; domain result -> DTO
    outbound/db/               async SQLAlchemy engine, `Base`, ORM models, repositories, DatabaseProbe
  application/
    ports/<tag>_port.py        Protocol the inbound adapter calls
    ports/health.py            ReadinessProbe protocol + NotReadyError (-> 503)
    ports/job_runs.py          JobRunStore, ports/clock.py Clock, ports/job_runner.py JobRunner
    services/job_registry.py   the list of scheduled jobs; services/run_due_jobs.py runs what is due
    services/<tag>_service.py  business logic (implements the port)
  domain/                      entities, value objects, errors; schedule.py, job_run.py
migrations/                    Alembic (async); versions/ is autogenerated
tests/
requirements.txt               runtime deps; requirements-dev.txt adds test/lint tooling
Dockerfile                     `dev` target (tools, bind mount) and `prod` target (slim)
compose.yaml                   app + postgres:16
dev.sh, scripts/               dev.sh dispatches to scripts/<command>.sh (template-owned)
service_scripts/               your own commands, same shape; dev.sh runs <command>.sh from here if scripts/ has none
tools/service_gen/             the code generator (template-owned; see Upgrading)
.github/workflows/ci.yml       GitHub Actions: ./dev.sh check + prod image build (template-owned)
```

Request flow: `Connexion -> generated handler -> request adapter -> port -> service -> domain`.

## Adding or changing an endpoint

1. Edit `openapi/openapi.yml` (every operation needs an `operationId` and a tag).
2. `./dev.sh sync` — regenerates `{{ package }}/generated/`, creates stubs for new
   tags/operations, and never touches files you already own.
3. If a new tag appeared, add its adapter to `build_adapters()` in `{{ package }}/main.py`.
4. Implement the service method (unimplemented ones return HTTP 501).

## Persistence

1. Add an ORM model subclassing `Base` under `{{ package }}/adapters/outbound/db/` and
   import it in `db/__init__.py` so Alembic sees it.
2. `./dev.sh revision "add widgets table"` then `./dev.sh migrate`.
3. Write a repository that takes the session factory, expose it via an outbound
   port (`application/ports/`), and hand it to the service in `build_adapters()`.

Raise `DomainError` subclasses from `{{ package }}/domain/errors.py` for business
failures; `{{ package }}/adapters/inbound/http_errors.py` maps them to RFC 7807 problem responses.

## Scheduled jobs

Declare a job in an inbound job adapter that calls a port your service implements, with its
schedule written as a five-field cron string in UTC (`parse_cron("0 0 * * *")`, from
`{{ package }}/adapters/inbound/cron.py`), and register the adapter's jobs in `register_jobs()` in
`{{ package }}/worker.py`. `./dev.sh run-due-jobs` runs whatever is due, once; in production something
outside the service fires `python -m {{ package }} run-due-jobs` (the `jobs` image target) or the
Lambda handler `{{ package }}.worker.handler` (`jobs-lambda`) on a clock, for example at midnight
UTC. Runs are recorded in the `job_runs` table. This service registers no jobs yet. Details: the
template's README, "Scheduled jobs".

## Health checks

A tag whose operations are all named like liveness/readiness probes (`livez`,
`healthz`, `readyz`, `ready`, ... with or without a `get` prefix) gets a working
service instead of 501 stubs. The service returns a `Health` object and the adapter
maps its `status` onto the response, so the response schema is expected to carry
`status`. Liveness always reports `ok`; readiness runs every `ReadinessProbe` from
`build_adapters()` in `main.py` and answers 503 on the first failure. `DatabaseProbe`
(`SELECT 1`) is wired by default; add a probe per external dependency.

## Upgrading the template

`scripts/`, `tools/service_gen/`, `dev.sh` and `.github/workflows/ci.yml` are owned
by the template. To pull a newer version (see `TEMPLATE_VERSION` for the current one):

```sh
./dev.sh upgrade            # latest main
./dev.sh upgrade v0.2.0     # a tag or branch
```

This replaces those paths, records the version, and runs `sync`. Review the
resulting diff as one PR; the release notes list any manual edits required in
files you own.

The template is fetched from the address in `TEMPLATE_REPO` (written by
`./dev.sh init`; set the `TEMPLATE_REPO` environment variable to override it).
Without that file, it is the template at the same host and scheme as this
repo's `origin`.
